<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">24 August in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 24 August, from 49 BCE to 1995.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juba-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/49-bce">49 BCE</a> <span class="tih-ago">2,074 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Juba I and the Pompeians destroy Curio's army on the Bagradas</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesars-civil-war">Caesar's Civil War</a>, the Pompeians under Publius Attius Varus and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a> routed the army of Caesar's general Gaius Scribonius Curio near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a> River, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Curio was killed in the battle.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesars-civil-war">Caesar's Civil War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">79 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,947 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vesuvius erupts, burying Pompeii and Herculaneum</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">By the traditional date, Mount Vesuvius erupted and buried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a> under ash and pumice, killing thousands. Some evidence suggests October; the ruins preserve a unique picture of Roman life.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">367 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,659 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Valentinian I makes his son Gratian co-Augustus</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a>, who ruled the West while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> held the East, named his eldest son Gratian, aged eight, co-Augustus. Gratian inherited the West on his father's death in 375.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/410">410 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,616 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Alaric's Visigoths sack Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> under Alaric entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and plundered it for three days, the first time the city had fallen to a foreign enemy in some 800 years. The shock prompted Augustine's City of God.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1200">1200 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">826 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">King John of England marries Isabella of Angoulême</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-angouleme">Isabella of Angoulême</a>, heiress of the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme-cathedral">Angoulême Cathedral</a>. The marriage provoked the Lusignans and helped bring on John's loss of Normandy.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">John, King of England</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-angouleme">Isabella of Angoulême</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme-cathedral">Angoulême Cathedral</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1456">1456 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">570 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The earliest dated copy of the Gutenberg Bible is finished</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The rubricator and binder Henricus Cremer finished his work on a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Gutenberg</a> Bible, the earliest dated copy known. The Bible was the earliest major book printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with mass-produced metal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing press</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1814">1814 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">212 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">British troops burn Washington</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After routing American militia at Bladensburg, British forces entered Washington and set fire to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> and the President's House. It was partly revenge for the American burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1932">1932 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">94 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Amelia Earhart flies non-stop across the United States</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Amelia Earhart flew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> to Newark in about 19 hours, the first woman to cross the continent non-stop. That May she had become the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1995">1995 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">31 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Microsoft launches Windows 95</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Microsoft released Windows 95 with a huge marketing campaign; customers queued at midnight. Its Start menu and taskbar set the pattern of the personal computer desktop for decades.</p></div></li></ol>
